drm/msm/dpu: keep the real interface count for DSC topologies

Linux 6.12 forces topology.num_intf = 1 whenever DSC is used. On a bonded
(dual) DSI panel with DSC the virtual encoder still has two physical
encoders, but the resource manager then reserves only one CTL. In
dpu_encoder_virt_atomic_mode_set() the second physical encoder gets no CTL,
the function bails out with "no ctl block assigned at idx: 1" and the next
dpu_crtc_atomic_begin() dereferences NULL (seen on TB371FC, R142 log).

Linux 7.1 counts the real interfaces for DSC as well (dpu_encoder_update_topology).
Keep intf_count here, so bonded DSI + DSC reserves two split-display CTLs,
two LMs and two DSC blocks - the same CTL layout 6.12 already uses for
bonded DSI without DSC.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_encoder.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_encoder.c
@@ -612,13 +612,15 @@ static struct msm_display_topology dpu_encoder_get_topology(
 	if (dsc) {
 		/*
-		 * In case of Display Stream Compression (DSC), we would use
-		 * 2 DSC encoders, 2 layer mixers and 1 interface
-		 * this is power optimal and can drive up to (including) 4k
-		 * screens
+		 * In case of Display Stream Compression (DSC), we use
+		 * 2 DSC encoders, 2 layer mixers and 1 or 2 interfaces.
+		 * A bonded DSI encoder has 2 physical encoders, each needs
+		 * its own CTL, so keep the real interface count (as 7.1 does).
 		 */
+		WARN(intf_count > 2,
+		     "DSC topology cannot support more than 2 interfaces\n");
 		topology.num_dsc = 2;
 		topology.num_lm = 2;
-		topology.num_intf = 1;
+		topology.num_intf = intf_count;
 	}

 	return topology;
